From 694ccac0802b02e59f7366c0f3febdf595b62c82 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bjorn Pagen <11238136+bjornpagen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:01:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH v1 2/4] pg_plan_advice: Add PARAMETERIZED and UNPARAMETERIZED
 advice.

Plan advice could pin a join's order, its method and the inner side's
index, but not whether the inner scan takes parameters from the outer
side, that is, whether the join clauses become conditions of the scan.
So two different plans generated the same advice: given

    JOIN_ORDER(d n) NESTED_LOOP_PLAIN(n) INDEX_SCAN(n n_pkey)

n might be probed through n_pkey once per row of d, or scanned through
n_pkey on its other columns alone with the join clause left as a join
filter.  When statistics make the two look equally cheap, as they do
after a VACUUM that ran while the transaction filling a table was still
open (pages counted, tuples not), the planner picks the latter, and the
advice generated from the former doesn't bring it back: add_path()
discards the parameterized path, which costs no less, before any join is
planned.  That breaks the module's promise that generated advice
reproduces the decision it was generated from.

PARAMETERIZED(n d) says that the scan of n takes its parameters from
exactly d, and PARAMETERIZED(n (d e)) from exactly d and e;
UNPARAMETERIZED(n) says that it takes none.  This is scan advice,
enforced from build_simple_rel_hook through the new
pgs_required_outer/pgs_forbidden_outer restriction: every path of n of
another parameterization is disabled, so it can no longer crowd out the
advised path in add_path(), and every join that would use it inherits
the disabled node.  Relations that supply parameters are named by their
topmost appendrel parents, as the planner names them, and outer joins
are never named.  Like join method advice, this implies a join order.

Generated advice includes the new items for each relation scanned by
itself for which the planner had a choice of parameterization (a
parameterized path was built, and another parameterization, possibly
none, was possible), much as SEMIJOIN_NON_UNIQUE is generated only where
unique-ification was considered.  The parameterization is read from the
final plan by following each NestLoop parameter back through the outer
side's targetlists to the scan that supplies it, as ruleutils does to
print it.  A scan that takes a computed parameter, such as a
PlaceHolderVar's value, gets no such advice, since the plan doesn't
record which relations the planner charged that value to.

test_plan_advice, which replans the whole core regression suite with
generated advice, passes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
