--- a/contrib/pg_plan_advice/README
+++ b/contrib/pg_plan_advice/README
@@ -199,6 +199,49 @@ technique should not be used. We should never assume that the absence of an
 instruction to do a certain thing means that it should not be done; all
 instructions must be explicit.
 
+Parameterization Advice
+=======================
+
+When a relation is scanned on the inner side of a nested loop, the planner
+can parameterize the scan by relations on the outer side, so that join
+clauses become conditions of the scan itself, typically index conditions,
+evaluated once per outer row. Which relations, if any, a scan takes its
+parameters from is a planner decision in its own right: join order, join
+method and index don't settle it. Given JOIN_ORDER(d n) NESTED_LOOP_PLAIN(n)
+INDEX_SCAN(n n_pkey), n might be probed by n_pkey once for each row of d, or
+scanned by n_pkey on its other columns alone with the join clause applied as
+a join filter, and both plans used to generate exactly that advice.
+
+PARAMETERIZED(n d) says that the scan of n takes its parameters from exactly
+d, and PARAMETERIZED(n (d e)) from exactly d and e together; UNPARAMETERIZED(n)
+says that it takes none. Relations that supply parameters are named by their
+topmost appendrel parents, because that is how the planner itself records a
+parameterization (until reparameterize_path_by_child() runs during plan
+creation), and outer joins are never named.
+
+This is scan advice, enforced when the target relation's paths are built: the
+module asks core, through RelOptInfo's pgs_required_outer and
+pgs_forbidden_outer, to disable every path of the relation whose
+parameterization differs. Enforcing it at the scan rather than at the join is
+essential. When the advised parameterized path costs no less than an
+unparameterized one, as happens when statistics say a table is empty,
+add_path() discards it before any join is planned, and no join-level advice
+could bring it back; once the unparameterized path is disabled, it can no
+longer crowd out the advised one. Like join method advice, parameterization
+advice implies a join order: the relations that supply the parameters must be
+joined before the parameterized one.
+
+We generate this advice only for relations for which the planner had a choice
+of parameterization: a parameterized path was built for the relation, and some
+other parameterization (possibly none) was also possible. A lateral reference
+forces a minimum parameterization; that is not a choice, and core never treats
+it as forbidden. The parameterization is read from the final plan, by
+following each NestLoop parameter back through the outer side's targetlists to
+the scan that supplies it. When a parameter's value is computed rather than
+read from a column, as a PlaceHolderVar's is, the plan doesn't record which
+relations the planner charged it to, so we generate no parameterization advice
+for the scan that uses it.
+
 Semijoin Uniqueness
 ===================
 
